  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nHigh pressure will be the dominant force the rest of today into\ntomorrow morning. This will provide for clear to mostly clear \nskies and a warm early October day. This has allowed for ample \nmixing, but the winds aloft are not high, so gusts have maxed \nout around 20 knots. There have been some diurnal cumulus as we \nhave warmed today, but this will clear out by this evening. \nTonight will have ample radiational cooling, which combined \nwith light winds will allow for some frost to form. The core of\nthis colder area will be in NWS DLH's area of NE Minnesota and \nNW Wisconsin but nearby areas of central Minnesota and west \ncentral Wisconsin could also see some frost. Due to this, the \npreviously issued Frost Advisory was continued. Temperatures \nshould rebound quickly tomorrow morning after sunrise.\n\nEarly this week will see a northwesterly flow aloft pattern \nwith few signs of any embedded waves and periods of WAA at 850 \nmb. This will keep us dry and sunny with warm temperatures. \nTemperatures look to have their early week peak on Tuesday as a \nwarm front passes. A mid week cold front passage will bring \ncooler, but still above normal temperatures. Late week into next\nweekend will see a building ridge aloft and the strongest WAA \nof the week. This will have peak temperatures on Saturday where \nhighs will be in the 70s with some lower 80s possible. With a \nstrong ridge there is little to no synoptic support for rain. \nNext Sunday into the following week looks to be where a larger \nshift could happen with colder air moving in and providing our \nnext chance for rain. Variability in guidance makes it hard to \nset the exact timing and impact, but the following week looks to\nbe more fall like. However how much cooler is uncertain as there\nare significant spreads with NBM max temperature fields ranging\n10 to 15 degrees for the 25th to 75th percentile spread. In \ncomparison most of this week see a spread of 2 to 5 degrees.",
